To center a `<div>` in CSS, you can use several methods depending on whether you want to center it horizontally, vertically, or both. Here are some common techniques:

### 1. Centering Horizontally

#### Using Margin Auto
If the `<div>` has a defined width, you can use `margin: auto` to center it horizontally.

```css
.center-div {
    width: 50%; /* or any specific width */
    margin: 0 auto; /* top/bottom margin 0, left/right auto */
}
```

### 2. Centering Vertically

#### Using Flexbox
Flexbox is a modern and flexible way to center elements both horizontally and vertically.

```css
.parent {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center; /* centers horizontally */
    align-items: center; /* centers vertically */
    height: 100vh; /* full viewport height */
}

.center-div {
    width: 50%; /* or any specific width */
}
```

### 3. Centering Both Horizontally and Vertically

#### Using Flexbox (as shown above)
You can use the same Flexbox method to center the `<div>` both ways.

#### Using Grid
CSS Grid can also be used for centering.

```css
.parent {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center; /* centers both horizontally and vertically */
    height: 100vh; /* full viewport height */
}

.center-div {
    width: 50%; /* or any specific width */
}
```

### 4. Centering with Absolute Positioning
If you want to center a `<div>` absolutely within a parent element, you can use the following method:

```css
.parent {
    position: relative; /* parent must have a position */
    height: 100vh; /* or any height */
}

.center-div {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%); /* adjust position */
}
```

### Summary
- Use `margin: auto` for horizontal centering with a defined width.
- Use Flexbox or Grid for both horizontal and vertical centering.
- Use absolute positioning with `transform` for precise control.

These methods are widely supported in modern browsers and are effective for centering elements in various layouts.

